  <h2>The linker script keeps GRUB happy</h2>
<pre><code><span class="c">// kernel/linker.ld — order matters; the multiboot2 header must be first</span>
SECTIONS &#123;
  . = 0x100000;                 <span class="c">// kernel loads at 1 MiB physical</span>
  .multiboot2_header : &#123; KEEP(*(.multiboot2_header)) &#125;   <span class="c">// in the first 32 KiB</span>
  .boot.text  : &#123; *(.boot.text) &#125;    <span class="c">// 32-bit entry, page tables, stacks</span>
  .boot.data  : &#123; *(.boot.data) &#125;    <span class="c">// boot GDT, GDTR</span>
  .text : &#123; *(.text*) &#125;
  .rodata : &#123; *(.rodata*) &#125;
  .data : &#123; *(.data*) &#125;
  .boot.pgtbl (NOLOAD) : &#123; *(.boot.pgtbl) &#125;  <span class="c">// zeroed by the loader (BSS-like)</span>
  .boot.stack (NOLOAD) : &#123; *(.boot.stack) &#125;  <span class="c">// 64 KiB boot stack</span>
  .bss : &#123; *(.bss*) &#125;
  <span class="c">// __kernel_end marks the end; used to compute kernel_size at boot</span>
&#125;</code></pre>
  <p>
    The <code class="mono">.boot.text</code>/<code class="mono">.boot.data</code>/<code class="mono">.boot.pgtbl</code>/<code class="mono">.boot.stack</code>
    sections exist so the 32-bit prologue and its zero-initialized tables sit near the start of the image and
    the loader's BSS-zeroing covers them. <code class="mono">.eh_frame</code> and
    <code class="mono">.note.GNU-stack</code> are discarded because unwinding metadata and an exec-stack note
    carry no meaning on bare metal.
  </p>

  <h2>Testing strategy</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Command</th><th>What it proves</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Format</td><td class="mono">cargo fmt --all -- --check</td><td>Style gates the diff.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Lint</td><td class="mono">cargo clippy -p kernel --target x86_64-unknown-none</td><td>Clippy needs the cross target explicitly — a classic trap.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Unit tests</td><td class="mono">cargo test -p kernel -- ipc::tests</td><td>Kernel data structures (IPC, capability, scheduler runqueue, timer) compile and test on the <strong>host target</strong>; <code class="mono">#[cfg(test)]</code> pulls in std and excludes the cross-compiled binary path. This is why <code class="mono">boot.rs</code> gates its <code class="mono">global_asm!</code> behind <code class="mono">#[cfg(target_os = "none")]</code> — a host link would otherwise clash with the C runtime's <code class="mono">_start</code>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Boot</td><td class="mono">./scripts/run.sh --test</td><td>Boots QEMU headless and greps serial for the "SIGRUN kernel running" banner; exit 0 iff found. The only end-to-end green light.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>GDB</td><td class="mono">./scripts/run.sh --gdb</td><td>Pauses QEMU at reset; attach with <code class="mono">gdb target/…/kernel -ex 'target remote :1234'</code> — the whole kernel has DWARF because it's cross-compiled with debug info.</td></tr>
  </table>
  <div class="callout">
    <span class="label">Why unit tests can run on the host at all</span>
    The test modules live inside <code class="mono">#![no_std]</code> crates, but under
    <code class="mono">cargo test</code> the crate is linked as a host binary with std available to tests —
    <code class="mono">alloc</code> works, atomics work, and the pure data structures (buddy lists, SPSC
    queue, capability rights) are architecture-independent. Only code touching ports, MMIO, or page tables is
    untested on host, which is what the QEMU boot test covers. That split — data structures on host, hardware
    in QEMU — is the entire test strategy.
  </div>

  <div class="qa">
    <h2>Interview Q&amp;A: build &amp; test</h2>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">CORE</span>Why nightly Rust, and which unstable features do you use?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          Three things: <code class="mono">global_asm!</code> for the boot code and the context switch,
          <code class="mono">#![feature(abi_x86_interrupt)]</code> for typed interrupt handlers, and stable
          inline <code class="mono">asm!</code> ergonomics that shipped later than the kernel started. The
          features are deliberately few and each is pinned to a real need — the "no speculative nightly
          features" rule is part of the project's correctness-over-speed stance.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="qa-followup"><b>FOLLOW-UP</b> "Could it build on stable?" — Mostly, by now: inline asm is stable, but <code class="mono">global_asm!</code> and the interrupt ABI are still nightly-gated, so the honest answer is "not without significant rewriting of the boot assembly."</div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">GOTCHA</span>Why does the build need <code class="mono">-C relocation-model=static -C code-model=kernel</code>?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          Two layers of the same problem: the compiler must not assume position-independence (there's no loader
          to relocate us — the linker places the kernel at 1&nbsp;MiB and that's final), and the 32-bit boot
          code needs absolute addresses that fit in 32 bits. lld expresses those as
          <code class="mono">R_X86_64_32</code> relocations and rejects them under the default PIE model, so the
          flags are what keep <code class="mono">lea eax, [_boot_pdpt]</code> linkable. Removing them is the
          fastest way to break the build on purpose.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">GOTCHA</span>Why is the ISO built with <code class="mono">i686-elf-grub</code> rather than the x86_64 package?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          The Homebrew <code class="mono">x86_64-elf-grub</code> builds only an EFI target; EFI GRUB can't load
          the BIOS/boot sector path that multiboot2 images expect from a legacy ISO. The
          <code class="mono">i686-elf-grub</code> package ships the <code class="mono">i386-pc</code> target with
          <code class="mono">grub-mkrescue</code>, which produces a BIOS-bootable ISO that QEMU's default
          SeaBIOS firmware runs. The same split explains why <code class="mono">qemu -kernel</code> won't work —
          it's the wrong boot contract for a 64-bit ELF.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="qa-item">
      <p class="qa-q"><span class="tag">DESIGN</span>What can't your test suite catch?</p>
      <div class="qa-a">
        <p>
          Everything that only runs at ring 0 with real hardware timing: TLB flush correctness under load,
          interrupt reentrancy races, the context switch's register discipline (a unit test can't watch a
          register), and MMIO behavior. That's why the QEMU boot test exists and why the emulator is the only
          regression gate. The next step in rigor would be Miri for the unsafe data structures (the roadmap
          lists it), then running the same ISO under KVM, then real hardware over serial.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

